              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TR7000iM (commercial)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$4,999, sold out</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">22 x 62 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5.0 HP continuous AC, 0.5–12 mph, −3 to 12 levels</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">400 lb</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            All prices, compare-at prices, stock status and specifications are from LifeSpan’s US store and
            product pages on 26 November 2026. The Amazon prices are from LifeSpan Fitness’s TX6 listings in
            black and white the same day. Amazon also has a TR5000B Pro+ GlowUp listing, but it showed no
            price, and its feature bullets give a 2.5 HP motor, a 350 lb capacity and a 0.6 mph minimum,
            which do not match LifeSpan’s own page. Trust LifeSpan’s figures, not that listing. We found no
            Amazon listing for the TR1200Pro or any LifeSpan desk set or folding treadmill.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two warnings about the under-desk figures. LifeSpan’s TR1200Pro page gives a 3.5 HP peak brushless
            motor in its specification table and a 3.0 HP continuous-duty motor in its feature list, so neither
            is a figure to compare across brands. And the folding treadmills have no compare-at price at all:
            the figures above are simply the last price LifeSpan attached to each before it ran out. Our <a href="/lifespan-tx6-under-desk-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">LifeSpan TX6-GlowUp review</a> goes further on the model
            most buyers end up choosing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="price-history" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What LifeSpan Treadmills Have Sold For</h2>
<p>
            LifeSpan’s under-desk prices have moved in $100 to $200 steps this autumn, mostly as models went in
            and out of stock. The prices recorded on our LifeSpan pages are a better guide to a fair price than
            the compare-at figures.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[560px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Model</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Earlier prices we recorded</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">26 Nov 2026</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TR1200Pro Glow-Up</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,099 on pre-order (LifeSpan, 14 Oct); $1,199 (LifeSpan, 24 Nov)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,199 LifeSpan</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TX6-GlowUp</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,299 (LifeSpan and Amazon, February 2026, per an independent tracker); $1,499 (LifeSpan, 14 Oct); $1,399 (LifeSpan, 5 Nov; LifeSpan and Amazon, 24 Nov)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,399 LifeSpan and Amazon</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TR5000B Pro+ GlowUp</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,699 on pre-order (LifeSpan, 14 Oct); $1,699 (Amazon, 9 Nov); $1,699 (LifeSpan, 24 Nov)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,699 LifeSpan</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Desk sets</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TR1200PRO Omni $2,048, Fit Desk $2,098, Desk Dash Pro $2,598, TR5000PRO Omni $2,748 (LifeSpan, 24 Nov)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Unchanged</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            <strong>The pattern:</strong> the compare-at prices of $1,299, $1,499 and $1,899 have not been what
            LifeSpan charged on any day we checked since mid-October; every under-desk model sold for $100 to $200
            less. The lowest figures on record are $1,099 for the TR1200Pro (on pre-order in October), $1,299 for
            the TX6 (February) and $1,699 for the TR5000B Pro+. Prices were the same on 26 November as on
            24 November, so there was no extra cut in Black Friday week. The full sets were unchanged too; our <a href="/how-much-does-a-treadmill-desk-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill desk cost guide</a> priced them on 24 November
            and set them against InMovement and iMovR.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="black-friday" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Is There a LifeSpan Black Friday Sale?</h2>
<p>
            Not one you could see on 26 November 2026. LifeSpan’s home page and product pages carried three
            banners that day: “TR1200 &amp; TR5000B Pro+ Are Back!”, “Free Shipping on $1,300 US Orders” and a
            note that its LifeSpan Fit app now syncs with Strava. None mentioned Black Friday, and the prices
            matched what we saw two days earlier. LifeSpan’s Sale page listed the TX6-GlowUp at $1,399 against
            $1,499 and the Desk Dash Pro at $2,598 against $2,689, the same discounts that were already running.
          </p>
          <p>
            The only Black Friday wording we found was in LifeSpan’s policies, and it is a restriction rather
            than a discount: orders placed between 25 November and 15 December can only be exchanged or
            refunded as store credit, and only until 15 January. The next section explains why that matters.
            A sale could still go live on the day itself, so if you are waiting, check the store on 27 November
            and compare the price with the history above. Our <a href="/treadmill-black-friday-deals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Black Friday treadmill deals</a> page covers this year’s offers
            across brands.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="step-up" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What Each Step Up Buys</h2>
<p>
            All three LifeSpan under-desk treadmills share a 20-inch-wide belt, six compression shocks, no
            incline and the same 10-year frame, 2-year parts and 1-year labour warranty. What changes is top
            speed, weight rating, the hours a day LifeSpan rates them for, and how high the belt sits off the
            floor.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[560px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Step</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Extra cost (26 Nov)</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">What you get, per LifeSpan</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TR1200Pro → TX6</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">+$200</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">6.0 mph instead of 4.0, 400 lb instead of 330, 7 hours a day instead of 6, a self-lubricating belt, and a remote and LED display (the TR1200Pro comes with LifeSpan’s Omni-Hub desk console instead); but a 5.8 in. step-up instead of 4.6</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TX6 → TR5000B Pro+</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">+$300</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">9 hours a day instead of 7, a 5.0 HP peak motor instead of 4.5, a 4.6 in. step-up, 84 lb instead of 98.5; but 4.0 mph top speed instead of 6.0</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TR1200Pro → TR5000B Pro+</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">+$500</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">400 lb instead of 330, 9 hours a day instead of 6, a heavier-duty belt; same 4.0 mph top speed and step-up</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>TR1200Pro to TX6 (+$200).</strong> This is the step most buyers should take. The extra
            70 lb of rated capacity and an hour more rated use a day are worth $200 on a machine that will run
            for years, and the TX6’s 6 mph ceiling lets you walk briskly away from the desk. The catch is height:
            at 5.8 inches the TX6 raises you 1.2 inches more than the TR1200, which eats into your standing
            desk’s range. LifeSpan also says the TX6 is limited to 4 mph when run from its Omni-Hub desk console.</li>
            <li><strong>TX6 to TR5000B Pro+ (+$300).</strong> This only makes sense for someone who walks at a
            steady desk pace for most of a long working day, or for an office where several people share one
            base. You give up 2 mph of top speed for two extra rated hours a day and a lower deck.</li>
            <li><strong>Staying with the TR1200Pro.</strong> If you weigh well under 330 lb, will walk for a few
            hours a day and never above 4 mph, it does the job for the least money. It was the cheapest LifeSpan
            treadmill you could order on 26 November, though it was on pre-order.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Against a budget walking pad, any LifeSpan is a large step up in price. Our guide to <a href="/under-desk-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-desk treadmills</a> sets out what the extra money buys in belt
            width, duty rating and warranty, and our <a href="/how-much-does-a-walking-pad-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad cost guide</a> shows what the cheaper end of
            the market charges.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="desk-sets" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Treadmill Desk Sets: Bundle or Separate?</h2>
<p>
            A LifeSpan desk set costs $2,048 to $2,748, and the main question is whether the bundle saves
            anything over buying a base and a desk on their own. On 26 November it mostly did not:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>TR1200PRO Omni, $2,048:</strong> exactly the TR1200Pro Glow-Up ($1,199) plus LifeSpan’s
            SD900 standing desk ($849). No saving, no extra cost.</li>
            <li><strong>Fit Desk, $2,098:</strong> exactly the TX6-GlowUp ($1,399) plus the SDG Glass standing
            desk ($699). Again no saving.</li>
            <li><strong>Desk Dash Pro, $2,598:</strong> LifeSpan describes the base as a TR5000 Glow Up with a
            4.5 HP motor, not the newer TR5000B Pro+. The Pro+ base ($1,699) and the same SDG Glass desk ($699)
            bought separately came to $2,398, which is $200 less, for the base with the higher 5.0 HP peak
            figure. Check which base you are getting before you choose the bundle.</li>
            <li><strong>TR5000PRO Omni, $2,748:</strong> a commercial base with an electric desk and the console
            built into the work surface. LifeSpan rates it for 9 hours a day and 400 lb.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The bundles do buy simplicity: one order, one delivery, and a desk and treadmill LifeSpan has
            matched for height. LifeSpan’s bundle pages quote delivery in 10 to 21 business days after shipping,
            longer than the 5 to 7 days quoted for a TX6 on its own. We covered the desks, heights and
            alternatives in detail on 24 November in our <a href="/how-much-does-a-treadmill-desk-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill desk cost guide</a>, and our <a href="/best-treadmill-desks/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmill desks</a> roundup compares LifeSpan with other makers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="delivery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Delivery and Shipping Costs</h2>
<p>
            LifeSpan’s site-wide banner on 26 November 2026 offered free shipping on US orders over $1,300. Every
            LifeSpan treadmill and desk set except the TR1200Pro Glow-Up ($1,199) clears that line on its own,
            so for most orders delivery adds nothing. On a TR1200Pro alone, the shipping cost is worked out at
            checkout from your address; LifeSpan does not publish a flat figure for it.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">What LifeSpan states</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Detail (26 Nov 2026)</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Free shipping</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">US orders over $1,300; not to Alaska, Hawaii, Puerto Rico or Canada</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Standard Delivery upgrade</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$149 (“rates may vary”): one or two people leave the order unassembled just outside your door; ground-level entry required</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Room of choice and assembly</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Mentioned in LifeSpan’s policies; no price published</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Shipping time</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">In-stock items ship in 1–5 days, usually delivered in 7–10 days, some up to 11–21; no expedited shipping</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TX6-GlowUp</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">“Shipped and delivered within 5–7 business days”</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TR1200Pro, TR5000B Pro+</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Pre-order; “shipping starts on Nov 25”</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            The under-desk treadmills arrive fully assembled, according to LifeSpan, so there is little to pay
            for beyond getting a roughly 80 to 100 lb box inside. LifeSpan gives the TR1200Pro at 77.61 lb, the
            TR5000B Pro+ at 84.13 lb and the TX6 at 98.5 lb. The desk sets need the desk built. Upgrade fees,
            if you pay any, are not refundable, and an upgrade must be requested before the order leaves the
            warehouse. Every LifeSpan base also needs a dedicated 115-volt outlet that is not GFCI-protected, per
            LifeSpan’s specifications, so check your wall before the box arrives.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="returns" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Return Policy Is Part of the Price</h2>
<p>
            This is the cost most LifeSpan buyers do not see coming. LifeSpan’s policy page says it stopped
            accepting 30-day returns on 1 February 2025. Every US order cancelled or returned after it has been
            marked as shipped, whether or not you have received it, is charged a 20% restocking fee and a $350
            return shipping fee, deducted from the refund. Refusing the delivery costs the same. Delivery and
            upgrade fees are not refunded, and LifeSpan says it will not accept returns for change of mind or
            for reasons such as size, weight or colour.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Model (26 Nov price)</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">20% restocking</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">+ $350 shipping</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Cost of a return</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TR1200Pro, $1,199</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$239.80</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$350</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$589.80</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TX6, $1,399</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$279.80</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$350</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$629.80</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TR5000B Pro+, $1,699</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$339.80</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$350</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$689.80</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TR1200PRO Omni set, $2,048</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$409.60</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$350</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$759.60</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Two more rules apply this week. Sale items, which on 26 November meant every under-desk treadmill,
            are eligible for return for credit or exchange only. And orders placed from 25 November to
            15 December are only eligible for exchange or store credit, until 15 January. Any return also needs
            LifeSpan’s written approval, the <a href="/how-to-pack-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>original packaging</a> and an unedited unboxing video recorded on
            delivery. Cancelling an order before it ships brings a full refund, per LifeSpan.
          </p>
          <p>
            Confusingly, LifeSpan’s product pages still show a “30 Day Return” badge. Go by the written policy,
            not the badge, and ask LifeSpan by email before you order if returns matter to you. Amazon sells the
            TX6 at the same $1,399; check the seller and return terms on the listing, which are set separately
            from LifeSpan’s store. Our guide to <a href="/treadmill-return-policies/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill return policies</a> compares other brands.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Warranty and Extended Protection</h2>
<p>
            Every LifeSpan treadmill on its store, under-desk, desk set or folding, lists the same warranty:
            10 years on the frame, 2 years on parts and 1 year on labour. None of the pages gives a separate
            motor term, so treat the motor as a part covered for two years. That cover is included in the price.
          </p>
          <p>
            LifeSpan sells extended plans through Extend, a third-party provider. Its <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>extended warranty</a> page
            lists 3-year and 4-year plans for its treadmills and desk sets, which stretch parts and labour cover
            to three or four years while the frame stays at ten. The plan’s price is shown at checkout and
            depends on the product; LifeSpan does not publish it. Extend’s FAQ on the same page says you can
            cancel within 60 days for a refund, less any paid claims.
          </p>
          <p>
            Is it worth it? The arithmetic depends on the plan price, which you will see only at checkout, but
            the logic is simple: the standard cover leaves the motor, belt and electronics unprotected from year
            three, and a desk treadmill can run for thousands of hours by then. If the plan costs a small share
            of the treadmill and you will walk for many hours every day, it buys real cover. Our <a href="/best-treadmills-with-long-warranty/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">longest-warranty treadmills</a> guide explains why parts
            and labour terms matter more than a long frame headline. Owner reports about LifeSpan’s service are
            mixed, as our <a href="/lifespan-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">LifeSpan brand guide</a> sets out, so keep your order
            confirmation and serial number somewhere safe.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-rivals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>LifeSpan vs Horizon, Sole and NordicTrack at the Same Price</h2>
<p>
            People searching for a LifeSpan treadmill sometimes want a home running machine, not a desk
            treadmill. Since LifeSpan’s folding models were all sold out, the fair comparison is between what
            LifeSpan does sell at each price and what the same money buys from the big running-treadmill brands,
            using the prices on our own cost pages.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[680px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Budget</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">LifeSpan</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Horizon</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Sole</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">NordicTrack</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About $1,100–$1,200</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TR1200Pro desk treadmill, $1,199</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">7.0 AT, $1,099 (Horizon, 23 Nov)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">F63, $1,099.99 (Sole, 22 Nov)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">—</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About $1,400–$1,500</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TX6 desk treadmill, $1,399</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">7.4 AT, $1,499.99 (Amazon, 23 Nov)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">F80, $1,499.99 (Sole, 22 Nov)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">—</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About $1,700</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TR5000B Pro+ desk treadmill, $1,699</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">7.4 AT, $1,799 (Horizon, 23 Nov)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">F83, $1,699.99 (Sole, 22 Nov)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">T Series 16, about $1,699 (Black Friday price, Nov 2026)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About $2,000</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TR3000i folding, last listed $1,999, sold out; or TR1200PRO Omni desk set, $2,048</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">7.8 AT, $1,999 (Horizon, 23 Nov)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">F85, $1,999.99 (Sole, 22 Nov)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Commercial 1750, about $1,999 (Black Friday price, Nov 2026), plus iFIT if wanted</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Read across a row and the difference is clear. At $1,099 to $1,199, Horizon’s 7.0 AT and Sole’s <a href="/sole-f63-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>F63</a> are full running treadmills with 20 by 60-inch decks, 12 mph and 15 levels of incline. LifeSpan’s
            TR1200Pro at the same price is a 4 mph desk walker with no incline. The LifeSpan earns its price in
            other ways: a low deck that fits under a desk, a 6-hour daily rating and a 330 lb limit. It is a
            different tool, not a cheaper or dearer version of the same one.
          </p>
          <p>
            When LifeSpan did sell folding running treadmills, they were not the value buys. The TR3000i’s last
            price of $1,999 bought a 20 by 56-inch belt and 12 mph; Sole’s F63 offers a 20 by 60-inch deck and
            12 mph for $1,099.99. Warranty points the same way: LifeSpan lists 10 years on the frame and 2 on parts,
            while Horizon and Sole both state lifetime frame and motor cover. NordicTrack adds an optional iFIT Pro
            membership at $396 a year, which LifeSpan, Horizon and Sole do not have. For a running treadmill, our <a href="/how-much-does-a-horizon-treadmill-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Horizon</a> and <a href="/how-much-does-a-sole-treadmill-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole</a> price guides are the better places to start.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="five-year-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Five-Year Cost of a LifeSpan</h2>
<p>
            LifeSpan does not charge a membership. Its treadmills sync with the LifeSpan Fit app, and neither the
            app page nor any treadmill page we read on 26 November mentions a subscription. So the five-year cost
            is close to the purchase price. The table assumes LifeSpan’s 26 November prices, free shipping where
            the order clears $1,300, no extended plan and no repairs, and divides by about 1,250 working days
            (five years of 250).
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[560px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Treadmill (26 Nov)</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Shipping</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Membership</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Five-year total</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Per working day</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TR1200Pro, $1,199</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Calculated at checkout</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$0</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,199 + shipping</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About $0.96 + shipping</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TX6, $1,399</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Free</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$0</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,399</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About $1.12</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TR5000B Pro+, $1,699</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Free</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$0</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,699</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About $1.36</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TR1200PRO Omni set, $2,048</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Free</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$0</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,048</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About $1.64</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TR5000PRO Omni set, $2,748</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Free</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$0</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,748</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About $2.20</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            The running costs on top are small. LifeSpan describes the TX6’s belt as self-lubricating and
            maintenance-free; for the other bases, LifeSpan sells its own belt lubricant at $17 (compare at
            $19.99), and a starter kit with a mat and lubricant at $79 to $89. Follow the <a href="/how-often-to-lubricate-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>lubrication interval</a> in your owner’s manual. The real wildcard is repairs after year two, when parts cover ends, which
            is where an extended plan or a careful choice of base comes in.
          </p>
          <p>
            Set against NordicTrack, the difference is the membership: five years of iFIT Pro at $396 a year is
            $1,980, more than a TX6 costs outright. Against Horizon and Sole, which also have no subscription,
            the five-year cost is simply the price. Our guide to <a href="/how-long-do-walking-pads-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how long walking pads last</a> covers the lifespan side of
            the sum for desk treadmills.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when-cheapest" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>When LifeSpan Treadmills Are Cheapest</h2>
<p>
            Going by the prices on our LifeSpan pages, the under-desk treadmills have sold $100 to $200 below
            their compare-at prices every time we have looked since October, and the price has not dropped
            further for Black Friday week. The lowest figures we have recorded are $1,099 for the TR1200Pro (on
            pre-order in October), $1,299 for the TX6 (February 2026) and $1,699 for the TR5000B Pro+. Treat those
            as the targets; at $1,199, $1,399 and $1,699 on 26 November, the TR5000B Pro+ was at its best price
            and the other two were $100 above theirs.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Cheapest LifeSpan you can order:</strong> the TR1200Pro Glow-Up at $1,199, on pre-order.</li>
            <li><strong>Cheapest in stock:</strong> the TX6-GlowUp at $1,399, on LifeSpan or Amazon.</li>
            <li><strong>Cheapest full treadmill desk:</strong> the TR1200PRO Omni at $2,048, the same as its
            parts bought separately.</li>
            <li><strong>Cheapest running treadmill:</strong> none; every LifeSpan folding model was sold out.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Because of the holiday return rule, buying between 25 November and 15 December costs you the right
            to a cash refund. If the price is the same either side of that window, as it has been, there is a
            case for ordering after 15 December, or for buying the TX6 from a seller whose returns you have
            read. Our <a href="/best-time-of-year-to-buy-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best time to buy a treadmill</a> guide covers
            the usual sale windows across brands.
          </p>
        </div>


        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Where People Go Wrong When Pricing a LifeSpan Treadmill</h2>
          <p>LifeSpan’s prices are straightforward. Its policies are where buyers lose money.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Trusting the “30 Day Return” badge</h3>
              <p>LifeSpan’s product pages still show it, but its written policy ended 30-day returns in February 2025 and charges 20% plus $350 on any return after shipping. Read the policy page, and ask LifeSpan by email before ordering if you are unsure.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Paying the compare-at price</h3>
              <p>Every under-desk LifeSpan has sold $100 to $200 below its compare-at price on every day we checked since October. If you see a full $1,299, $1,499 or $1,899, wait or check Amazon, which matched LifeSpan on the TX6.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Assuming a bundle saves money</h3>
              <p>The TR1200PRO Omni and Fit Desk cost exactly the same as their parts. The Desk Dash Pro cost $200 more than the newer TR5000B Pro+ base and the same desk bought separately. Add up the parts first.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Buying a desk walker to run on</h3>
              <p>LifeSpan’s current treadmills top out at 4 or 6 mph and have no incline. If you want to run, a Horizon 7.0 AT or Sole F63 costs less than a TX6 and is built for it.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">A LifeSpan treadmill costs $1,199 to $1,699, or $2,048 to $2,748 as a desk set.</strong> On 26 November 2026 LifeSpan had the TR1200Pro Glow-Up at $1,199, the TX6-GlowUp at $1,399 (also
            $1,399 on Amazon) and the TR5000B Pro+ at $1,699, with free US shipping over $1,300 and no membership.
            Its folding running treadmills were all sold out.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">Price the return policy in before you buy.</strong> A return after shipping costs
            20% plus $350, and holiday orders are exchange or credit only. For most desk walkers the TX6 is the one
            to choose; see our <a href="/lifespan-tx6-under-desk-treadmill/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">LifeSpan TX6-GlowUp review</a> before you order.
          </p>
        </div>

